│ -**Figure 4 — the 36-arm scatter.** Each point is one single-cell arm on the k=24 (cutwA) base from the L175 scan (`banc/l175/results_l175.json`): x is the cell's mean rate x̄ on that base, drawn on a log10 scale with a floor at 10⁻⁴ (values at or below the floor, including the numerically ≈0 silents, sit at the left edge); y is the mean response meanR over the 16 right-side seeds (kR/16 is not shown — meanR carries the band edges). Filled small dots are active cells (x̄ > 0.05), hollow dots silent cells; the carrier DNp33-L (gi 1110, arm sc25) is the large highlighted marker — the only arm past the dashed SUPER threshold (meanR ≥ 0.60 with kR ≥ 12); the four twin families get distinct shapes: square = mass twin gi 319 (silent, hollow), triangles = drive-matched dmA/dmC (gi 511/1480), diamond = signed-current gi 1508 (s2t, DNg19). The gray horizontal band is the cutwA base itself (5/16, meanR 0.408, Wilson 95% CI 0.142–0.556; the fresh-batch base 0.370 is the dashed line inside it). Open markers with dashed ties add the L185 fresh-batch (b40, rematched-gain) values for the six key arms (sc25, sc26, sc27, sc40, dmA, dmC); the s2t diamond exists only in b40 — no values were missing, so no marker was omitted. Reading: rate is not destiny — seven cells are driven harder than the carrier (1.5–4.2×) and none flips (arrow); the carrier's 16/16 SUPER replicates at 15/16 (meanR 0.770), while every twin — matched for mass, drive, or signed current — lands inside the base band. All numbers come only from the two stored results files; nothing was recomputed or smoothed. PNG rendered from the SVG with rsvg-convert.
